@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

@code{mp} is a user-facing Octave class. An object owns its represented
MPFR/MPC value, shape, and stored precision. Public values are immutable at
the native boundary; destructive MPLAPACK calls receive operation-owned
copies. The package does not silently route supported numerical work through
builtin binary64 arithmetic.

Use this package when the input data, intermediate values, or requested
tolerances require more precision than binary64 provides. Use ordinary
Octave values when binary64 is sufficient or when a deferred API is needed.

@node Installation and package loading
@chapter Installation and package loading

Install the source package with Octave's package manager:

@verbatim
pkg install mplapack-interop-0.5.0-dev.tar.gz
pkg load mplapack-interop
@end verbatim

For a local dependency installation, the repository helper starts a configured
session at @file{/home/docker/opt/octave-mplapack-stack/bin/octave-mplapack}.
After starting Octave, load the package with:

@verbatim
pkg load mplapack-interop
@end verbatim

The package requires GNU Octave 11.1 or newer and an installed MPLAPACK MPFR
interface discoverable through @code{pkg-config}. The package does not vendor
MPLAPACK or search a developer-specific source path. Check the active stack
with @code{mplapack_version ()}.

@node Creating mp values
@chapter Creating @code{mp} values

Decimal text is parsed directly at the current default precision:

@verbatim
mpbits (256);
x = mp ("0.1");
A = mp ({"1.0", "2.0"; "3.0", "4.0"});
@end verbatim

A builtin double has already been rounded before @code{mp} receives it:

@verbatim
binary64_value = mp (0.1);
decimal_value = mp ("0.1");
@end verbatim

These values intentionally preserve different source information. A complex
scalar can be made directly from two decimal strings:

@verbatim
z = mp ("1.25", "-0.5");
@end verbatim

Dense matrices are two-dimensional and have one uniform stored precision.
Use @code{char}, @code{disp}, and explicit @code{double} when inspecting or
converting values. Only the last of these is an intentional binary64
conversion.

@node Precision model
@chapter Precision model

@code{mpbits ()} reports the project default; @code{mpbits (p)} changes it
for subsequently constructed values. @code{mpdigits (d)} requests
@code{ceil(d*log2(10))} bits. A fresh process starts at 512 bits.

Changing the default does not recreate information already lost:

@verbatim
mpbits (128);
low = mp ("0.1");
mpbits (1024);
high = mp ("0.1");
% low remains a 128-bit value; high is newly constructed at 1024 bits.
@end verbatim

Existing operands determine the operation precision. For two @code{mp}
operands it is the maximum stored precision; a builtin double is converted
at the @code{mp} operand precision. The ambient default does not retroactively
change operands or select an arithmetic result precision.

Linear-algebra and other native calls enter one operation-owned precision
scope, normalize temporaries to that precision, and restore the caller's
thread-local backend context. Real-to-complex promotion happens once at the
selected precision. Guard precision is used only where the function-specific
documentation says so; it is not a hidden binary64 fallback.

@node Real and complex values
@chapter Real and complex values

Real and complex values share the public @code{mp} class but have different
native payload kinds. @code{real}, @code{imag}, @code{conj}, @code{isreal},
@code{abs}, @code{arg}, and @code{angle} inspect or transform those payloads
using MPFR/MPC operations.

A real input is kept on a real path when the operation permits it. A result is
promoted to complex @code{mp} when the mathematical branch requires a complex
value, for example a negative real square root or a general real matrix with
complex eigenvalues. A builtin complex double may be an input, but it is
converted into the operation precision. A raw builtin-double-only operation
remains an Octave operation outside this package's contract.

@node Arithmetic and comparisons
@chapter Arithmetic and comparisons

The element-wise arithmetic functions and operators are @code{plus},
@code{minus}, @code{times}, @code{rdivide}, @code{uplus}, @code{uminus},
@code{power}, and @code{mpower}. Dense multiplication uses @code{mtimes} and
MPLAPACK @code{Rgemm} or @code{Cgemm}. Mixed real/complex inputs promote to
MPC at one operation precision.

Comparisons use native MPFR/MPC equality and the documented complex
magnitude/phase ordering for supported extrema and sorting operations.
Ordered comparisons on complex values remain rejected.

@verbatim
mpbits (512);
a = mp ("0.1");
b = mp ("0.2");
c = a + b;
d = a .* b;
e = c ^ 2;
@end verbatim

@node Dense matrices and indexing
@chapter Dense matrices and indexing

The dense public type is two-dimensional, column-major, and contiguous at the
native boundary. @code{size}, @code{rows}, @code{columns}, @code{numel},
@code{ndims}, @code{length}, and @code{isempty} inspect shape without changing
stored precision.

@code{subsref}, @code{subsasgn}, and @code{end} provide normal dense indexing
and value-semantic in-bounds assignment. @code{reshape}, @code{transpose},
@code{ctranspose}, @code{horzcat}, @code{vertcat}, and @code{cat} preserve
native values. @code{diag}, @code{triu}, @code{tril}, @code{repmat},
@code{flip}, @code{fliplr}, @code{flipud}, and @code{rot90} are supported for
the dense two-dimensional forms. @code{zeros}, @code{ones}, @code{eye},
@code{NaN}, and @code{Inf} support @code{"like"} construction with an
@code{mp} template.

Sparse, N-dimensional, deletion, growth assignment, and packed triangular
forms are not part of the public dense contract.

@node Matrix factorizations and linear solves
@chapter Matrix factorizations and linear solves

Use @code{A \ b} or @code{A / B} instead of explicitly forming an inverse for
a solve. Square left division uses @code{Rgesv} or @code{Cgesv}; rectangular
and rank-revealing forms use @code{Rgelss} or @code{Cgelsy}. Right division
uses the conjugate-transpose solve identity and preserves complex
conjugation.

@code{chol} uses @code{Rpotrf}/@code{Cpotrf}; @code{qr} uses
@code{Rgeqrf}/@code{Rorgqr} or @code{Cgeqrf}/@code{Cungqr}; pivoted QR uses
@code{Rgeqp3}/@code{Cgeqp3}; and @code{lu} uses @code{Rgetrf}/@code{Cgetrf}.
Every destructive call receives an operation-owned copy.

@verbatim
A = mp ({"4", "2"; "2", "10"});
b = mp ({"1"; "2"});
x = A \ b;
[Q, R] = qr (A);
[L, U, P] = lu (A);
@end verbatim

Result tolerances should be based on the stored operation precision and the
conditioning of the problem, not on a fixed binary64 epsilon.

@node Eigenvalues SVD Schur QZ
@chapter Eigenvalues, SVD, Schur and QZ

@code{eig} has structured, general, and generalized forms. Structured real
symmetric and complex Hermitian inputs use @code{Rsyevd}/@code{Cheevd}.
General problems use @code{Rgeevx}/@code{Cgeevx}. Generalized definite and QZ
forms use @code{Rsygvd}/@code{Chegvd} and @code{Rggev}/@code{Cggev}.

Eigenvalue order is not canonical. Eigenvector scale and complex phase are
not canonical either, so validate residuals rather than elementwise vectors.
For a standard problem use @code{A*V = V*D}; for a generalized problem use
@code{A*V = B*V*D}. Three-output forms return left vectors and should be
checked with the corresponding conjugate-transpose identity.

The required Grcar example is:

@verbatim
mpbits (1024);
A = mp (gallery ("grcar", 32));
[V, D] = eig (A);
r = norm (A*V - V*D, "fro") / norm (A, "fro");
disp (r);
@end verbatim

@code{schur}, @code{hess}, and @code{qz} have non-unique factors. QZ
generalized eigenvalues may be represented by an @code{alpha}/@code{beta}
pair; @code{beta == 0} represents an infinite eigenvalue. Reconstruction
orientation is part of the API contract and is documented in
@file{docs/eig.md} and @file{docs/generalized-eig.md}.

@code{svd}, @code{pinv}, @code{null}, @code{orth}, and @code{rref} are
sensitive to singular-value tolerances. Singular-vector signs/phases are
non-unique; use reconstruction, orthogonality, and projector residuals.

@verbatim
n = 8;
H = mp (zeros (n, n));
for i = 1:n
  for j = 1:n
    H(i,j) = mp (1) / mp (i + j - 1);
  endfor
endfor
[U, S, V] = svd (H);
sv = diag (S);
svd_residual = norm (H - U*S*V', "fro") / norm (H, "fro");
disp (sv);
disp (svd_residual);
@end verbatim

@node Matrix functions
@chapter Matrix functions

@code{expm}, @code{logm}, and @code{sqrtm} use package-owned arbitrary-
precision matrix algorithms with MPFR/MPC working values. Principal branches
and branch cuts can promote a real input to complex. Validate matrix
identities and inspect conditioning near a branch cut.

@code{mpower} for integer square matrices uses exponentiation by squaring;
noninteger matrix powers are deferred. @code{polyvalm} evaluates a polynomial
at a dense matrix.

@node Elementary and special functions
@chapter Elementary and special functions

The supported scalar/element-wise elementary family includes
@code{sqrt}, @code{cbrt}, @code{exp}, @code{expm1}, @code{log},
@code{log1p}, @code{log10}, @code{log2}, @code{sin}, @code{cos},
@code{tan}, @code{asin}, @code{acos}, @code{atan}, @code{sinh},
@code{cosh}, @code{tanh}, @code{asinh}, @code{acosh}, @code{atanh},
@code{hypot}, and @code{atan2}. Real domain crossings use the documented
MPC promotion rules.

The supported MPFR-backed special family is @code{gamma}, @code{gammaln},
@code{lgamma}, @code{erf}, and @code{erfc}. Poles, signed zeros, infinities,
NaNs, and domain branches are tested explicitly. Other special-function
families are deferred and rejected cleanly.

@node Polynomial operations
@chapter Polynomial operations

@code{poly}, @code{polyval}, @code{polyvalm}, @code{roots}, @code{conv},
@code{deconv}, @code{polyder}, @code{polyint}, and @code{compan} operate on
arbitrary-precision coefficient or root data. Polynomial roots are
conditioning-sensitive; clustered roots can move materially when precision
changes. Use residuals such as @code{polyval (p, roots (p))} and not only
elementwise root matching.

@verbatim
p = mp ({"1", "-3", "2"});
r = roots (p);
residual = norm (polyval (p, r));
disp (residual);
@end verbatim

@code{polyfit} and @code{polyeig} remain deferred.

@node Set operations
@chapter Set operations

@code{unique}, @code{union}, @code{intersect}, @code{setdiff},
@code{setxor}, and @code{ismember} support the documented dense real/complex
forms, including stable and row options where implemented. Equality and
ordering use native values; NaN membership follows the documented Octave
compatibility rule. @code{ismembertol} is deferred.

@node Serialization and save/load
@chapter Serialization and save/load

The @code{saveobj} and @code{loadobj} hooks use schema
@code{octave-mplapack-mp}, version 1. The schema records kind, shape, stored
precision, and canonical text elements in column-major order. It preserves
finite values, signed zero, infinity, NaN classification, and complex
components without raw pointers or MPFR limb dumps.

@verbatim
mpbits (1024);
A = mp ({"1.234567890123456789", "-0"; "Inf", "NaN"});
save ("/tmp/mp-example.mat", "A");
clear A;
load ("/tmp/mp-example.mat");
disp (A);
@end verbatim

The package must be loaded before loading an @code{mp} object. Malformed or
newer schemas are rejected rather than guessed.

@node Graphics
@chapter Graphics

@code{plot}, @code{plot3}, @code{loglog}, @code{semilogx}, @code{semilogy},
@code{scatter}, @code{scatter3}, @code{stem}, @code{stairs}, @code{mesh},
@code{surf}, @code{contour3}, @code{meshc}, @code{surfc}, and @code{waterfall}
are boundary wrappers. They explicitly convert only final visualization data
to builtin @code{double}; all numerical calculation before plotting remains
MPFR/MPC. This is the documented exception to the no-binary64-fallback rule.

@verbatim
t = linspace (mp ("0"), mp ("6.283185307179586476925286766559"), 32);
y = sin (t);
plot (double (t), double (y));
@end verbatim

For a package @code{mp} graphics call, the wrapper performs the conversion.
For values outside the binary64 range, transform or scale in @code{mp} before
the graphics boundary. N-D and volume graphics, and @code{meshgrid} for
@code{mp}, are deferred.

@node Arbitrary-precision random numbers
@chapter Arbitrary-precision random numbers

@code{mprand}, @code{mprandn}, and @code{mprandi} use the package's
@code{xorshift128plus-v1} engine and generate p-bit values directly. They are
not cryptographic. @code{mprng} controls the seed and serializable state.
Fixed seed/state is required for reproducibility.

@verbatim
mprng ("seed", uint64 (7));
u1 = mprand (2, 1);
mprng ("seed", uint64 (7));
u2 = mprand (2, 1);
assert (isequal (u1, u2));
@end verbatim

@node Interpolation
@chapter Interpolation

@code{interp1} and @code{interp2} use MPFR/MPC grids, query points, and
piecewise-polynomial values. @code{pchip}, @code{spline}, @code{mkpp},
@code{unmkpp}, @code{ppval}, @code{ppder}, and @code{ppint} operate on the
supported scalar/vector piecewise-polynomial contract. Query-grid precision
matters: a query that collapses to a double grid point may remain distinct at
MPFR precision. Extrapolation is explicit. @code{interp3}, @code{interpn},
and matrix-valued PP output are deferred.

@node Nonlinear equations
@chapter Nonlinear equations

@code{fzero} accepts a scalar start or sign-changing bracket; @code{fsolve}
accepts real scalar/vector starts. Callbacks receive @code{mp} values and must
return @code{mp} values. @code{TolX}, @code{TolFun}, finite-difference steps,
and stopping checks are precision-aware. Complex nonlinear callbacks are
deferred.

@verbatim
mpbits (512);
target = mp ("1.25");
[root, fval, info] = fzero (@(x) x*x - target*target, mp ({"0", "2"}));
assert (info == 1);
assert (abs (root - target) < mp ("1e-40"));
@end verbatim

@node Numerical integration
@chapter Numerical integration

@code{integral} and @code{quadgk} use MP nodes, weights, transforms, and
error estimates. Scalar callbacks may return real or complex @code{mp};
builtin double and array-valued callback results are rejected. Finite and
infinite intervals, waypoints, and precision-aware absolute/relative
tolerances are supported. @code{ArrayValued} remains deferred.

@verbatim
options = struct ("AbsTol", mp ("1e-40"), "RelTol", mp ("1e-40"));
[value, err] = integral (@(x) x*x, mp ("0"), mp ("1"), options);
assert (abs (value - mp ("1")/mp ("3")) < mp ("1e-35"));
@end verbatim

@node Optimization
@chapter Optimization

@code{fminbnd} uses an MPFR safeguarded golden-section search and
@code{fminsearch} uses an MPFR Nelder--Mead simplex. Objective callbacks must
return finite real @code{mp} scalars. @code{TolX}, @code{TolFun}, iteration
limits, and evaluation limits are precision-aware. @code{fminunc} remains
deferred.

@node Performance notes
@chapter Performance notes

Higher precision increases scalar arithmetic cost and can increase workspace
and serialization size. Dense algorithm dimension scaling remains that of the
selected algorithm. MPLAPACK-backed calls and package-owned MPFR/MPC
algorithms have different constant factors. RNG generation cost grows with
requested precision. Graphics deliberately loses arbitrary precision at the
final display boundary.

@node Octave compatibility
@chapter Octave compatibility

The supported surface follows ordinary Octave syntax where the dense
two-dimensional @code{mp} contract permits it. The authoritative compatibility
boundaries are @file{docs/octave-script-compatibility.md} and
@file{docs/advanced-numerics-compatibility.md}. Unsupported calls involving
@code{mp} fail through package-owned diagnostics rather than silently
delegating to builtin binary64 implementations.

For exact supported forms, consult @file{docs/public-api-inventory.md}. The
public package name is @code{mplapack-interop}; the public numeric class is
@code{mp}. Native @code{__mplapack_core__} operations are implementation
details and are not user API.

@node Deferred and unsupported functionality
@chapter Deferred and unsupported functionality

The following are intentionally deferred: optional Schur/QZ ordering helpers,
@code{funm}, @code{polyfit}, @code{polyeig}, @code{ismembertol}, remaining
special-function families, @code{meshgrid} for @code{mp}, volume/N-D graphics,
matrix-valued PP output, @code{interp3}, @code{interpn}, complex nonlinear
callbacks, @code{ArrayValued} quadrature, and @code{fminunc}. Sparse,
symbolic, N-D, and unrelated ODE/PDE surfaces are also outside the current
contract.

Each deferred item has a re-entry record under @file{docs/todo/}; a missing
form must not be implemented by a builtin-double fallback. These limitations
are part of the release documentation, not hidden omissions.

@node Troubleshooting
@chapter Troubleshooting

@table @asis
@item @code{pkg load} fails
Confirm the package is installed, the package name is
@code{mplapack-interop}, and Octave's package directory is on its path.

@item MPLAPACK runtime is not found
Check @code{pkg-config --modversion mplapack_mpfr}, the installed library
search path, and the runtime environment used to start Octave. Do not add a
developer source directory as a workaround.

@item Precision unexpectedly looks low
Construct from decimal text, set @code{mpbits} before construction, and
remember that changing the default cannot restore information lost by a
previous binary64 constructor.

@item Version or dependency mismatch
Use @code{mplapack_version ()} and @code{pkg-config}. D04 currently records
MPLAPACK 3.0.1 as a release candidate, not a completed release.

@item Graphics overflow or underflow
Perform scaling or logarithms in @code{mp}, then explicitly convert the final
display data.

@item Callback or serialization error
Return @code{mp} from nonlinear/integration/optimization callbacks and load
the package before restoring an @code{mp} object. Check schema version and
stored precision.
@end table

